<Vertical id="ChoiceInputCodes">
  <Markdown>
### Coding a Likert pair

Each option carries a `code=` — the number the response is **recorded** as, in
the survey sense (the SPSS code, the Qualtrics recode value). It is not a
score, not points, and not a grade.

The second item is **reverse-coded**: agreeing with it means the opposite of
agreeing with the first. It says so on the item — `reverseCoded="true"` — and
also spells the reversed numbers out, so the reversal lives in the content
rather than in whoever reads the data later. A sign flip in either the
attribute or one of the codes would make the two disagree, and the parser
would say so.
  </Markdown>

  <ChoiceInput id="codes_growth">
    <Key id="codes_growth_strongly_agree" value="strongly_agree" code="2">Strongly agree</Key>
    <Key id="codes_growth_agree" value="agree" code="1">Agree</Key>
    <Key id="codes_growth_disagree" value="disagree" code="-1">Disagree</Key>
    <Key id="codes_growth_strongly_disagree" value="strongly_disagree" code="-2">Strongly disagree</Key>
  </ChoiceInput>
  <Markdown>*I can get better at writing if I work at it.* — recorded as **{{@codes_growth.code}}**</Markdown>

  <ChoiceInput id="codes_fixed" reverseCoded="true">
    <Key id="codes_fixed_strongly_agree" value="strongly_agree" code="-2">Strongly agree</Key>
    <Key id="codes_fixed_agree" value="agree" code="-1">Agree</Key>
    <Key id="codes_fixed_disagree" value="disagree" code="1">Disagree</Key>
    <Key id="codes_fixed_strongly_disagree" value="strongly_disagree" code="2">Strongly disagree</Key>
  </ChoiceInput>
  <Markdown>*Either you are a writer or you are not.* (reversed) — recorded as **{{@codes_fixed.code}}**</Markdown>

  <Markdown>
### The default table

These options carry no `code=` at all. Their values are ones the default-code
table knows, so the codes come from there.
  </Markdown>

  <ChoiceInput id="codes_defaulted">
    <Key id="codes_defaulted_true" value="true">True</Key>
    <Distractor id="codes_defaulted_false" value="false">False</Distractor>
  </ChoiceInput>
  <Markdown>Recorded as **{{@codes_defaulted.code}}** (no `code=` was written).</Markdown>

  <Markdown>
### A reversal with no codes at all

`reverseCoded="true"` negates the table for this item's options, so the
shortest honest way to write a reversed Likert item is to write no `code=`
anywhere and say once that the item is reversed.
  </Markdown>

  <ChoiceInput id="codes_reversed_defaulted" reverseCoded="true">
    <Key id="codes_reversed_defaulted_strongly_agree" value="strongly_agree">Strongly agree</Key>
    <Key id="codes_reversed_defaulted_agree" value="agree">Agree</Key>
    <Key id="codes_reversed_defaulted_neutral" value="neutral">Neutral</Key>
    <Key id="codes_reversed_defaulted_disagree" value="disagree">Disagree</Key>
    <Key id="codes_reversed_defaulted_strongly_disagree" value="strongly_disagree">Strongly disagree</Key>
  </ChoiceInput>
  <Markdown>*I am no good at this and never will be.* (reverse-coded) — recorded as **{{@codes_reversed_defaulted.code}}**</Markdown>
</Vertical>
